# Line endings are decided here, in the index, and not by whoever committed last.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows and runs on Linux, and had no rule
# until now: whether a file was stored CRLF or LF depended on what `core.autocrlf`
# happened to be when someone added it. A `mixed-line-ending` pre-commit hook was
# tried first and was the wrong tool -- it rewrites the *working tree*, which
# fights autocrlf and turned a six-file change into a 117-file one.
#
# `text=auto` stores text as LF and checks it out however the platform wants,
# which is the same guarantee applied in the right place. Blobs committed as CRLF
# before this file existed are left alone deliberately: git compares them
# normalized, so they no longer show as modified, and rewriting a hundred files
# to fix nothing observable would cost more history than it buys.
* text=auto

# These are read by Linux, always, and a CR is not whitespace to a kernel.
# A shell script with CRLF fails as `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`,
# which names the interpreter rather than the line ending and sends everyone
# looking in the wrong place. The container in `Dockerfile` runs these.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Justfile        text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf

# Generated and never hand-edited. Marking it keeps a lockfile bump from
# dominating the diff view on a pull request.
uv.lock         linguist-generated=true -diff

# Excalidraw is JSON that its editor rewrites wholesale; diffing it helps nobody.
*.excalidraw    -diff
